        "fontFamily": "sans-serif",
        "baseline": 464.938,
        "fontSize": 9.5
      },
      {
        "text": ", the density of an object may help identify its composition. The density of gold,",
        "x": 232.574,
        "y": 456.472423828125,
        "width": 307.4179955000001,
        "height": 10.5205078125,
        "fontFamily": "serif",
        "baseline": 464.938,
        "fontSize": 9.5
      },
      {
        "text": "for example, is about 2.5 times the density of iron, which is about 2.5 times the density of aluminum. Density also reveals",
        "x": 72,
        "y": 467.87242382812497,
        "width": 467.96630450000026,
        "height": 10.5205078125,
        "fontFamily": "serif",
        "baseline": 476.33799999999997,
        "fontSize": 9.5
      },
      {
        "text": "something about the phase of the matter and its substructure. Notice that the densities of liquids and solids are roughly",
        "x": 72,
        "y": 479.27242382812494,
        "width": 467.99199250000026,
        "height": 10.5205078125,
        "fontFamily": "serif",
        "baseline": 487.73799999999994,
        "fontSize": 9.5
      },
      {
        "text": "comparable, consistent with the fact that their atoms are in close contact. The densities of gases are much less than those of",
        "x": 72,
        "y": 490.6724238281249,
        "width": 467.99991550000004,
        "height": 10.5205078125,
        "fontFamily": "serif",
        "baseline": 499.1379999999999,
        "fontSize": 9.5
      },
      {
        "text": "liquids and solids, because the atoms in gases are separated by large amounts of empty space. The gases are displayed for a",
        "x": 72,
